   "html_text": "Ockham himself, writing 1321–1323, raises an array of arguments against Scotus’s position. Ockham was a thinker explicitly influenced by Scotus in all sorts of ways, while nevertheless rejecting the fundamental feature of Scotist metaphysics and semantics, namely realism on the question of common natures. Without exception, the arguments target one of the following two Scotist positions: the commonality of the nature, and the formal distinction. As such, Ockham does not attack haecceities, thoug",
